Patch 55. After patch 54.

ImGuiDrawer wrote the mouse and touch state into ImGui directly (io.MousePos, io.MouseDown,
io.MouseWheel), so ImGui saw only the state at each NewFrame. A press and a release that arrive
between the same two frames (an instant tap, like Android's `input tap`, or a click faster than a
frame) left the button up at the next frame: ImGui never saw the press and the click was lost. On
Android the dialogs get touches as SDL's synthesized mouse events, so a tap on a message box's OK
did nothing.

The handlers now queue ImGui input events (AddMousePosEvent, AddMouseButtonEvent,
AddMouseWheelEvent, AddMouseSourceEvent), the API ImGui has had since 1.87 (the keys already used
it). The queue keeps every transition and spreads them over the following frames in order
(ConfigInputTrickleEventQueue), so a tap without movement is a click however short it is. Mouse
capture follows the drawer's own record of the buttons held, as io.MouseDown now changes only at
NewFrame; detaching clears the queue too, so no event replays into the next activation. A press
that the other kind of pointer interrupts (a touch while a mouse button is held, or the reverse) is
released away from everything, so it is abandoned rather than completed as a click.

Two consequences beyond the tap. On macOS ImGui's default ConfigMacOSXBehaviors now applies to the
mouse too: Cmd+click is a right click in the dialogs (the direct writes bypassed it). And the
mouse events go through ImGui's queue (a vector) like the key events already did, so the input
handlers and Draw must stay on one thread, as they are when a presenter draws the dialogs and in
this project's detached use.

Test: tests/unit/ui/imgui_drawer_input_test.cpp drives a modal with an OK button through a window's
input listeners: a click and an instant touch tap between two frames press it once each, without
moving the dialog; a click held over several frames presses it once; a press released outside it
does not; a press interrupted by the other pointer clicks nothing; the window captures the mouse
only while a button is held, also when the last dialog goes with a button down. Without the change
the instant cases fail (0 presses). Verified by the unit test only, not yet with a tap on a device.

Not explained by this: in one Android run an instant tap moved a message box down by about 615 px
instead of pressing OK; neither the lost press nor gamepad navigation moves a window, and it was not
seen again.

The generated code does not change; codegen_id does (src and include are among its inputs).

Upstream: not proposed.

